  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A cake consists of NNN pieces arranged in a row. Each piece has one of MMM distinct flavours (numbered 111 through MMM) which are available in Chef's kitchen. For each valid iii, the cost of the iii-th flavour is iii rupees.\n\nWhen Chef bakes a cake, he chooses the flavour of each piece independently and uniformly randomly from these MMM flavours. After baking the cake, he splits it into several contiguous segments (each piece must belong to exactly one segment) and sells each segment to a different customer.\n\nThe price of a segment is the sum of costs of all distinct flavours of the pieces in that segment. For example, the cost of a segment with flavours (1,2,1,5)(1, 2, 1, 5)(1,2,1,5) is 1+2+5=81+2+5=81+2+5=8. Chef's profit is the sum of prices of all segments. Chef is very greedy, so he wants to maximise his profit, but he is also a perfectionist, so he wants to split the cake into the minimum number of segments such that his profit is still the largest possible.\n\nChef is wondering about the number of customers he would be able to serve. Help him find the expected value of the number of segments he would split a cake into.\n\nIt can be proved that this expected value can be expressed as a fraction p/qp/qp/q, where ppp and qqq are positive integers and qqq is coprime to 109+710^9+7109+7. You should find p⋅q−1p \\cdot q^{-1}p⋅q−1 modulo 109+710^9+7109+7, where q−1q^{-1}q−1 denotes the multiplicative inverse of qqq modulo 109+710^9+7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2⋅1051 \\le T \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤T≤2⋅105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤M≤1091 \\le M \\le 10^91≤M≤109",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer p⋅q−1p \\cdot q^{-1}p⋅q−1 modulo 109+710^9+7109+7.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n4 2\n5 6",
        "output": "875000009\n948302478",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1:\r\nFor the following sequences of flavours, the cake can be divided into:\n\n2 segments: (1,2,1,2),(1,2,2,1),(2,1,1,2),(2,1,2,1)(1,2,1,2),(1,2,2,1),(2,1,1,2),(2,1,2,1)(1,2,1,2),(1,2,2,1),(2,1,1,2),(2,1,2,1)\n3 segments: (1,1,1,2),(1,1,2,1),(1,1,2,2),(1,2,1,1),(1,2,2,2),(2,1,1,1),(2,1,2,2),(2,2,1,1),(2,2,1,2),(2,2,2,1)(1,1,1,2),(1,1,2,1),(1,1,2,2),(1,2,1,1),(1,2,2,2),(2,1,1,1),(2,1,2,2),(2,2,1,1),(2,2,1,2),(2,2,2,1)(1,1,1,2),(1,1,2,1),(1,1,2,2),(1,2,1,1),(1,2,2,2),(2,1,1,1),(2,1,2,2),(2,2,1,1),(2,2,1,2),(2,2,2,1)\n4 segments: (1,1,1,1),(2,2,2,2)(1,1,1,1),(2,2,2,2)(1,1,1,1),(2,2,2,2)\n\nThe expected value of the number of segments is (2⋅4+3⋅10+4⋅2)/16=23/8(2 \\cdot 4 + 3 \\cdot 10 + 4 \\cdot 2) / 16 = 23/8(2⋅4+3⋅10+4⋅2)/16=23/8."
      }
    ]
  },
